5.4.3. Initializing an ECS Cluster (EC2 Launch Type) &amp; ECS Capacity Provider An Amazon ECS Cluster is a logical grouping used to manage Docker Containers. By connecting this Cluster to the Auto Scaling Group (ASG) created in section 5.4.2 via a Capacity Provider, ECS gains the authority to automatically request new EC2 instances when containers need more resources (RAM/CPU) to handle a massive influx of orders.
Step 1: Initialize the ECS Cluster Navigate to the ECS (Elastic Container Service) console on AWS. In the left menu, select Clusters and click the Create cluster button. Under Cluster configuration: Cluster name: Eshop-ECS-Cluster Under Infrastructure: AWS Fargate (Serverless) is selected by default. However, our architecture uses EC2 to optimize costs according to project requirements. Check the box for Amazon EC2 instances. As soon as you select EC2, the Auto Scaling group (ASG) section will appear. Select Eshop-ECS-ASG (the ASG we created in section 5.4.2) from the dropdown list. Selecting the ASG directly here allows AWS to automatically create a Capacity Provider for you. Scroll to the bottom and click Create. Step 2: Verify the Capacity Provider and EC2 Instances Once the Cluster is successfully created (which takes about 1-2 minutes), we need to confirm that ECS has recognized the EC2 instances as its &ldquo;workers.&rdquo;
Click on the Eshop-ECS-Cluster name to enter its details page. Switch to the Infrastructure tab. Scroll down to the Capacity providers section. You should see a newly auto-created provider (usually sharing the name of the ASG, with an Active status). Scroll further down to the Container instances section. You should see 2 EC2 instances with an Active status (These are the 2 servers provisioned by the ASG in section 5.4.2, which have successfully registered themselves into the ECS Cluster). Your Backend server cluster infrastructure is now ready! In the next section, we will set up the Load Balancer &ldquo;Gateway&rdquo; to direct customers into this cluster.
